// **`true`/`false` are keywords, not identifiers**, so an attribute argument that is a bool needs
// its own arm in `parse_attr_args`. Found by migrating `#![repository(Patient, crud: false)]` to
// `#![derive(Repository(Patient, crud: false))]`: the hand-written parser matched `TOK_FALSE`
// directly and the generic reader refused the same text outright, with "an attribute argument is a
// string, a whole number or a name".
//
// Its kind is `bool` and its text is `true` or `false`, so a generator compares against the text
// as it does for every other kind -- and a spec can ask for one (`cache:bool?`).
//
// The refusal below is the point of recording the kind at all: the lexer would hand over the same
// text for `#[min("8")]` and `#[min(8)]`, and `#[min(true)]` is a third thing that is not a number.
struct E {
    #[min(true)]
    count: int
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
